{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 327 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "70dc52dc0a0e8d26dc635382d1f3ca182cd68ef4b2fe82a14fd743eca57920e5" :edb/db-before-t 326 :edb/db-after-t 327 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533639 50 #inst "2026-10-04T05:21:24.086Z" 13194139533639 true] [17592186068757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243378 13194139533639 true] [17592186068757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243406 13194139533639 true] [17592186068757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243413 13194139533639 true] [17592186068757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243420 13194139533639 true] [17592186068758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243385 13194139533639 true] [17592186068758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243392 13194139533639 true] [17592186068758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243427 13194139533639 true] [17592186068759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243364 13194139533639 true] [17592186068759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243399 13194139533639 true] [17592186068759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243434 13194139533639 true] [17592186068759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243441 13194139533639 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243357 13194139533639 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243371 13194139533639 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243448 13194139533639 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243455 13194139533639 true] [17592186069119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533639 true] [17592186069119 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186069120 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533639 true] [17592186069120 1145 "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/1e7f53108e1bbfee39ed331ea4b0d7954b8bdf98310ebe2376008835b908bbbe.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/99d164fc056b13447a549abffd210bb4338e36ec6fe67d148daa5bb68a9a07a5.png)" 13194139533639 true] [17592186069121 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533639 true] [17592186069121 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ hence $c=4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}+4 & =6 \\\\ k+4 & =6 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=2-4=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186069156 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533639 true] [17592186069156 1145 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186069157 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533639 true] [17592186069157 1145 "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243358 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1089 "q-170347" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1113 #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1138 false 13194139533639 true] [17592186243357 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243359 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243360 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243361 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243362 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243363 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1041 "selection" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243361 13194139533639 true] [17592186243358 1110 #uuid "d558351d-dd08-592d-a83d-147f0983afeb" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243359 1034 "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [17592186243359 1111 #uuid "e8e94192-12b8-5fb3-9b2c-24ad6146199e" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243360 1034 "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243360 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [17592186243360 1111 #uuid "4950a90f-d990-5237-a730-339e2f778a72" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243361 1034 "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [17592186243361 1111 #uuid "1da6a60b-7ed8-5f86-ac74-a08fceabbe36" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243362 1034 "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [17592186243362 1111 #uuid "1a121cb3-97ae-5f85-88a5-b2c1c582ccf9" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243363 1034 "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [17592186243363 1111 #uuid "4700703f-8be8-59d3-ab77-8245ecd49b77" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243365 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1089 "q-170350" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/06ea604e70390627f9e98dbea5048f27bd1f63d0b10002bbd5518c000b5125bf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1113 #uuid "8624b867-0c46-5fb9-bd50-4d5ac46cd2c3" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1138 false 13194139533639 true] [17592186243364 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243366 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243367 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243368 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243369 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243370 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1041 "selection" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243366 13194139533639 true] [17592186243365 1110 #uuid "0b43d465-a0b4-535f-8262-fab967b6ad8c" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243366 1034 "-\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [17592186243366 1111 #uuid "a40fc808-0e04-5dbc-af3a-696d4fcf0822" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243367 1034 "2" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533639 true] [