7c30956218a00da51e77db950183bd2a85dbe74107e493fd5f03b.png" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533639 true] [17592186243397 1111 #uuid "a0d71e5a-4fb7-5a97-a732-e8a1fecd8ab3" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243398 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/f63e49a2e8f4a0702841eb132093bf6f69b8405af25719ea8f5abbf2bd691122.png" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533639 true] [17592186243398 1111 #uuid "8496a3b4-ffda-51b1-aa50-e5b36497c0a8" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243400 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1089 "q-27542" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/2a76eaba665b5d7c777ca794ca213bbdc55dfb46b94b49c71679e2c20910add7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1113 #uuid "8f13e5d3-ff2f-52ec-88f0-51b21c8f112c" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1138 false 13194139533639 true] [17592186243399 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5.$ Hence, $c=2.5.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2.75),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2.75$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.5 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+2.5 & =2.75 \\\\ k+2.5 & =2.75 \\\\ k & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=0.25-2.5=-2.25.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=0.25{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.5.$" 13194139533639 true] [17592186243400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243401 13194139533639 true] [17592186243400 1000 #edb/ref 1759